<body>

<h1>ArgusNet &mdash; Production Features Roadmap</h1>
<p class="subtitle">All 20 features below are implementable with Laravel (PHP). Each entry includes what it does and how to build it.</p>

<p class="feature-title">1. &nbsp; Scheduled Auto-Polling (Background Jobs)</p>
<p class="row"><span class="label">What:</span> &nbsp; Automatically ping all targets every N minutes without any user action &mdash; even when no browser is open.</p>
<p class="row"><span class="label">How:</span> &nbsp; Laravel Scheduler (<code>app/Console/Kernel.php</code>) + a custom Artisan command (<code>php artisan ping:all</code>). Add the cron entry <code>* * * * * php artisan schedule:run</code> on the server. Store the interval per-target or globally in a settings table.</p>

<p class="feature-title">2. &nbsp; Email Alerts on Status Change</p>
<p class="row"><span class="label">What:</span> &nbsp; Send an email when a target goes from Online &rarr; Offline (down alert) or Offline &rarr; Online (recovery alert).</p>
<p class="row"><span class="label">How:</span> &nbsp; Laravel Mailables + <code>Mail::to()-&gt;send()</code>. Compare the new ping result against the last recorded <code>is_success</code> in <code>ping_histories</code>. Queue the mail with <code>Mail::queue()</code> so it does not block the ping loop. Use SMTP, Mailgun, or SendGrid via <code>.env</code> config.</p>

<p class="feature-title">3. &nbsp; User Authentication</p>
<p class="row"><span class="label">What:</span> &nbsp; Require login to access the dashboard &mdash; prevent unauthorized users from seeing your network map.</p>
<p class="row"><span class="label">How:</span> &nbsp; Laravel Breeze or Jetstream for scaffolding. Add <code>auth</code> middleware to all routes in <code>web.php</code>. Provides login, logout, and password reset out of the box.</p>

<p class="feature-title">4. &nbsp; Role-Based Access Control (RBAC)</p>
<p class="row"><span class="label">What:</span> &nbsp; Admin users can add/delete targets and change settings; Viewer users can only watch the dashboard.</p>
<p class="row"><span class="label">How:</span> &nbsp; Add a <code>role</code> column (<code>admin</code> / <code>viewer</code>) to the <code>users</code> table. Create a <code>CheckRole</code> middleware that blocks non-admins from POST/PUT/DELETE routes. Or use the <code>spatie/laravel-permission</code> package for more granular permissions.</p>

<p class="feature-title">5. &nbsp; Device Groups and Tags</p>
<p class="row"><span class="label">What:</span> &nbsp; Organize targets into groups (e.g. Servers, Switches, Printers, DMZ) to filter and monitor by category.</p>
<p class="row"><span class="label">How:</span> &nbsp; Create a <code>groups</code> table and a pivot table <code>group_target</code>. Add a <code>GroupController</code> with CRUD. Add a group filter dropdown in the frontend. Use Eloquent <code>belongsToMany</code> relationship.</p>

<p class="feature-title">6. &nbsp; HTTP / HTTPS Endpoint Monitoring</p>
<p class="row"><span class="label">What:</span> &nbsp; Monitor websites and APIs by checking their HTTP status code (200, 500, etc.) and response time &mdash; not just ICMP ping.</p>
<p class="row"><span class="label">How:</span> &nbsp; Add a <code>type</code> column to targets (<code>icmp</code> / <code>http</code>). For HTTP targets, use Laravel&rsquo;s HTTP client: <code>Http::timeout(5)-&gt;get($url)</code>. Record the status code and response time in <code>ping_histories</code>. Add a <code>status_code</code> column to the table.</p>

<p class="feature-title">7. &nbsp; TCP Port Monitoring</p>
<p class="row"><span class="label">What:</span> &nbsp; Check whether a specific port is open on a host (e.g. port 22 for SSH, 3306 for MySQL, 5432 for PostgreSQL).</p>
<p class="row"><span class="label">How:</span> &nbsp; Add <code>port</code> and <code>type</code> columns to targets. For TCP targets, use <code>fsockopen($host, $port, $errno, $errstr, 3)</code> inside <code>performPing()</code>. Record success/failure and connection time in milliseconds.</p>

<p class="feature-title">8. &nbsp; Latency Threshold Alerts</p>
<p class="row"><span class="label">What:</span> &nbsp; Alert when a target&rsquo;s response time exceeds a configured threshold (e.g. warn if latency &gt; 100ms, critical if &gt; 500ms).</p>
<p class="row"><span class="label">How:</span> &nbsp; Add <code>warn_ms</code> and <code>critical_ms</code> columns to targets. After each ping, compare <code>response_time</code> against thresholds and trigger a notification if exceeded. Store an alert state flag per target to avoid sending repeated alerts every minute.</p>

<p class="feature-title">9. &nbsp; Maintenance Mode per Target</p>
<p class="row"><span class="label">What:</span> &nbsp; Pause monitoring for a specific target during planned maintenance so you do not receive false-alarm alerts.</p>
<p class="row"><span class="label">How:</span> &nbsp; Add an <code>is_paused</code> boolean column to targets. In the ping logic (Artisan command and manual ping), skip targets where <code>is_paused = true</code>. Show a &ldquo;Paused&rdquo; badge in the UI and add pause/resume action buttons.</p>

<p class="feature-title">10. &nbsp; Downtime Log and Incident Tracking</p>
<p class="row"><span class="label">What:</span> &nbsp; Automatically record every outage as an incident &mdash; when it started, when it ended, and total duration.</p>
<p class="row"><span class="label">How:</span> &nbsp; Create an <code>incidents</code> table with: <code>target_id</code>, <code>started_at</code>, <code>resolved_at</code>, <code>duration_seconds</code>. When a ping fails and the previous was success, open a new incident. When ping recovers, close it and calculate duration. Add an Incidents page in the React frontend.</p>

<p class="feature-title">11. &nbsp; SLA Reports (Uptime Reports by Period)</p>
<p class="row"><span class="label">What:</span> &nbsp; Generate uptime/downtime reports for a given date range (daily, weekly, monthly) &mdash; useful for reporting to management.</p>
<p class="row"><span class="label">How:</span> &nbsp; Add a <code>ReportController</code> with a <code>generate()</code> method. Query <code>ping_histories</code> filtered by date range, calculate uptime%, total downtime in minutes, and number of incidents. Return as JSON or as a downloadable PDF using the <code>barryvdh/laravel-dompdf</code> package.</p>

<p class="feature-title">12. &nbsp; Bulk Import via CSV</p>
<p class="row"><span class="label">What:</span> &nbsp; Add dozens of targets at once by uploading a CSV file (<code>name, ip_address, group</code>) instead of adding them one by one.</p>
<p class="row"><span class="label">How:</span> &nbsp; Create an <code>ImportController</code> that accepts a file upload. Use Laravel&rsquo;s <code>Storage</code> + <code>str_getcsv()</code> or the <code>maatwebsite/excel</code> package to parse rows. Validate each row and bulk-insert with <code>Target::insert()</code>. Return a summary of created vs. failed rows.</p>

<p class="feature-title">13. &nbsp; Subnet Scanner</p>
<p class="row"><span class="label">What:</span> &nbsp; Scan a whole subnet (e.g. 192.168.1.0/24) and auto-discover live hosts, then let the user add them as targets.</p>
<p class="row"><span class="label">How:</span> &nbsp; Create an Artisan command <code>php artisan scan:subnet {cidr}</code>. Loop through all IPs in the range using <code>ip2long</code> / <code>long2ip</code>. Ping each IP with <code>performPing()</code>. Return the list of live IPs as JSON. The frontend shows discovered hosts with checkboxes to add them as targets.</p>

<p class="feature-title">14. &nbsp; API Tokens for External Access</p>
<p class="row"><span class="label">What:</span> &nbsp; Allow external scripts, tools, or dashboards to pull your monitoring data via a secure API key.</p>
<p class="row"><span class="label">How:</span> &nbsp; Use Laravel Sanctum (<code>php artisan sanctum:install</code>). Protect API routes with <code>auth:sanctum</code> middleware. Users can generate and revoke tokens from their profile page. External tools send <code>Authorization: Bearer &lt;token&gt;</code> in request headers.</p>

<p class="feature-title">15. &nbsp; Webhook Notifications (Slack / Teams / Discord)</p>
<p class="row"><span class="label">What:</span> &nbsp; Send real-time alerts to a Slack channel, Microsoft Teams, or Discord server when a host goes down or recovers.</p>
<p class="row"><span class="label">How:</span> &nbsp; Add a <code>webhook_url</code> field to targets or a global settings table. When an alert triggers, use <code>Http::post($webhookUrl, $payload)</code>. For Slack use Incoming Webhooks JSON; for Teams use Adaptive Card format; for Discord use their embed format. Queue with <code>dispatch()</code> for async delivery.</p>

<p class="feature-title">16. &nbsp; Notification History Log</p>
<p class="row"><span class="label">What:</span> &nbsp; Keep a record of every alert that was sent &mdash; channel used, message content, time, and target &mdash; for auditing.</p>
<p class="row"><span class="label">How:</span> &nbsp; Create a <code>notifications_log</code> table with: <code>target_id</code>, <code>channel</code> (email / slack / webhook), <code>message</code>, <code>sent_at</code>. After every notification dispatch, insert a row. Add a &ldquo;Notification Log&rdquo; page in the frontend with filters by target and date range.</p>

<p class="feature-title">17. &nbsp; Per-Target Check Intervals</p>
<p class="row"><span class="label">What:</span> &nbsp; Set a different polling frequency for each target &mdash; e.g. check critical servers every 1 minute but printers every 10 minutes.</p>
<p class="row"><span class="label">How:</span> &nbsp; Add a <code>check_interval_minutes</code> column to targets (default: 5). In the Artisan scheduler command, only ping a target if <code>now() &gt;= last_ping_at + interval</code>. Use a single <code>schedule-&gt;everyMinute()</code> loop that evaluates each target&rsquo;s individual interval independently.</p>

<p class="feature-title">18. &nbsp; Device Notes and Documentation</p>
<p class="row"><span class="label">What:</span> &nbsp; Attach notes to each target &mdash; physical location, responsible team, rack position, credentials reference, and more.</p>
<p class="row"><span class="label">How:</span> &nbsp; Add a <code>notes</code> text column to targets. Add a Notes field in the Edit modal. Display it as a collapsible section in the target detail view. Supports basic Markdown rendered client-side with a lightweight JS library like <code>marked</code>.</p>

<p class="feature-title">19. &nbsp; Dashboard Widgets and Customizable Layout</p>
<p class="row"><span class="label">What:</span> &nbsp; Let users pin or hide stat cards, choose which table columns are visible, and save their layout preferences.</p>
<p class="row"><span class="label">How:</span> &nbsp; Store layout config as JSON in a <code>user_preferences</code> table. A <code>PreferenceController</code> handles GET/PUT requests for server-side persistence. The React dashboard reads the config on load and renders only the selected widgets and columns.</p>

<p class="feature-title">20. &nbsp; Two-Factor Authentication (2FA)</p>
<p class="row"><span class="label">What:</span> &nbsp; Add an extra layer of security &mdash; users must enter a TOTP code from an authenticator app in addition to their password.</p>
<p class="row"><span class="label">How:</span> &nbsp; Use the <code>pragmarx/google2fa-laravel</code> package. After login, redirect to a 2FA challenge page. Store the TOTP secret encrypted in the <code>users</code> table. Works with Google Authenticator, Authy, and any TOTP-compatible mobile app.</p>

<p class="footer">Total: 20 features &mdash; all implementable with standard Laravel features, official packages, or PHP built-in functions.</p>

</body>
</html>
